<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mahmud Hotak</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1697">1697</a>–<a href="/years/1725">1725</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Mir Mahmud Hotak</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mahmud Hotak was an Afghan ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghilzai">Ghilzai</a> Hotak dynasty, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirwais-hotak">Mirwais Hotak</a>. In 1722 he defeated the Safavid army at Gulnabad and besieged and captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>, forcing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan-husayn">Shah Sultan Husayn</a> to abdicate. This effectively ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mahmud-hotak.jpg" alt="Mahmud Hotak" width="320" height="164" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: M.samei · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mahmud_Hotak_Coin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mahmud Hotak was born in 1697, the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirwais-hotak">Mirwais Hotak</a>, chief of the Ghilji Pashtuns, who made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a> region independent of Safavid rule in 1709. When Mirwais died in 1715 his brother Abdul Aziz succeeded him and returned to Safavid suzerainty, which was unpopular among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a>. Mahmud gathered his father's loyal followers, entered the royal palace, killed Abdul Aziz himself and took the throne at the age of 18.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Mahmud began to wage war on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a> in 1720 with a siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerman">Kerman</a>. In October 1721 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghilzai">Ghilzais</a> took the town but not its citadel, and lost one thousand five hundred men because they lacked experience of siege tactics. In 1722 he marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a> with 20,000 men, bypassing Kerman and being fended off near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazd">Yazd</a>. On 8 March 1722 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a> met the Persian army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-gulnabad">Battle of Gulnabad</a> and, though outnumbered and worse equipped, routed it.</p><p>They then besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>, beginning with the capture of Julfa. Lacking siege equipment, they took until 23 October 1722, and it is believed that over 80,000 of the city's inhabitants died. Shah Soltan Hoseyn went to the Afghan camp at Farahabad, took off his crown and placed it on Mahmud's turban. Mahmud at first treated the royal family well and brought food to the starving capital, and his forces took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a> and Kashan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In November 1722 Hoseyn's son Tahmasp declared himself shah. Mahmud's army took Qazvin, but its people rose against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pashtuns">Pashtuns</a> in January 1723. Fearing revolt, Mahmud invited Persian ministers and nobles to a meeting and had them killed, and executed up to 3,000 of the Persian royal guards. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> and Russians seized land while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> was in chaos. In February 1725 he ordered the other Safavid princes put to death, sparing two young children when Hoseyn tried to stop the killing.</p><p>His health and reason failed. On 22 April 1725 Afghan officers freed his cousin Ashraf from prison and placed him on the throne. Mahmud was killed by Ashraf, though other sources say he died of his insanity. He was twenty-seven. Afterwards the Ghilji chiefs, who had served as kalantars of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a>, rebuilt the bureaucracy, and Mirza Sami'a wrote the Tazkerat al-Moluk, a manual of how the state was administered.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mahmud Hotak” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Afghan independence had a forerunner: in 1709 Mirwais Hotak, chief of the Ghilji tribe, broke from the Safavids, and his son <b>Mahmud Hotak</b> ruled much of Iran from 1722 to 1725, until Nader Shah Afshar toppled the Hotaks in 1738.</blockquote><a href="/empire/durrani-empire">Durrani Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After surrendering Isfahan in 1722, Shah Sultan Husayn personally placed the crown on the head of the Afghan conqueror <b>Mir Mahmud Hotak</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>His son <b>Mahmud Hotak</b> invaded Persia, defeated the Safavid army at Gulnabad in 1722 and took Isfahan after a long siege, forcing Shah Sultan Husayn to abdicate.</blockquote><a href="/state/hotak-dynasty">Hotak dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Khorasan Province, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.3,59.6&amp;year=1715" aria-label="Khorasan Province on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Mahmud Hotak"><image href="/maps/globes/g060n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4972 4462h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4972 4462h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=36.3,59.6&amp;year=1715">What was here in 1715? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
